<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Zealand</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New Zealand is an island country in the south-western Pacific, colonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from the nineteenth century. It became one of the self-governing settler territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>, later a dominion. Its status illustrates the diverse constitutional arrangements under which British territories were governed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-zealand.jpg" alt="New Zealand" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Albert Hastings Markham Vector: Zscout370, Hugh Jass, s. File history · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_New_Zealand.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The self-governing dominions of Canada (1867), Australia (1901), <b>New Zealand</b> and South Africa (1910) had elected parliaments and substantial autonomy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…first self-governing dominion of the British Empire, and its gradual, negotiated path from colony to independence, through responsible government, Confederation and the Statute of Westminster, became a model for Australia, <b>New Zealand</b> and South Africa and for the later transformation of the empire into the Commonwealth of Nations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Anzac tradition shaped national identity in both Australia and <b>New Zealand</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The population rose from 20,019 at the first census in 1900 to about 100,000 today, while from the 1960s many Tongans emigrated to <b>New Zealand</b>, Australia and the United States.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tonga">Kingdom of Tonga · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Malaysian Armed Forces grew from the Malay Regiment, founded in 1933, and from units raised during the Malayan Emergency, and for its first years the federation relied heavily on Britain, Australia and <b>New Zealand</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>New Zealand</b> adopted the Statute of Westminster only in 1947, sixteen years after Britain passed it, and created its own citizenship from 1 January 1949.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
